        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Natural sources of radiation in space include galactic cosmic rays (GCR), solar energetic particles, and trapped energetic particles in a planetary magnetic field. These sources are difficult to shield because of their high energies and dense ionization patterns, thus posing significant health risks to astronauts on long-term space missions. Efforts to protect astronauts from harmful cosmic radiation require a deeper understanding of the effects of GCR on human health. In particular little is known about the effects of GCR exposure on the hematopoietic stem cell (HSC) population and whether disrupted genetic stability in HSCs could result in the development of hematopoietic malignancies in astronauts on deep space missions. 
<p>
Investigators studied the effects of high-linear energy transfer (LET) radiation characteristic of GCR on hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells in vivo and determined changes in HSC behavior that may predispose to leukemia and lymphoma development. Of importance is the fact that the average age of a crewmember has risen above 46 years, and their work and others have shown that HSCs display diminished function with age. Recent data from their group has demonstrated that middle-aged individuals show frequent defects in DNA mismatch repair in HSCs. Investigators used DNA repair deficient mouse models to study the effects of heavy ion radiation on HSCs that represent HSCs of potential astronaut populations, and determine whether exposure to high charge and energy (HZE) particles and the additional delta rays produced in their traversal through matter will disrupt the hematopoietic niche. <p>
APPROACH:<br>
To determine if Mlh1 deficiency affects HSC functions, investigators irradiated bone marrow (BM) cells collected from Mlh1+/+ and Mlh1+/- mice with 0, 10, 50, 100, or 250 cGy of 1H ion or 28Si ion. BM cells were plated to form myeloid colony forming units (CFU) and counted in order to determine impact of IR and Mlh1 status of HSCs. Investigators hypothesized that due to Mlh1+/- mice showing a significantly higher and early incidence of lymphomagenesis in 56Fe ion irradiated mice, the impact of 1H ion or 28Si ion would be similar. Post IR, they followed mice up to 18 months and sacrificed any mouse with a visible sign of distress or palpable tumor.
<p>
To determine gene expression profile of each T-cell rich B-cell lymphoma cohorts, the investigators decided to run RNA-sequencing analysis. The RNA-sequencing experiment was carried out to detect differentially expressed genes and to determine which signaling pathways are involved in the process of tumorigenesis. To study this, they collected RNAs from snap frozen T-cell–rich B-cell (TRB) lymphomas arising from Mlh1+/+ and Mlh1+/- mice irradiated with sham-, 1H ions, or 28Si ions. To identify differentially expressed genes, each TRB lymphoma gene expression profile was compared to Mlh1+/+ and Mlh1+/- lymph nodes collected from healthy age-matched sham-irradiated mice.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The investigators discovered that high-LET 28Si ion caused more damage to BM cells and hence a significantly less myeloid CFU compared to 1H ion irradiated BM cells. As anticipated, Mlh1 status did not affect acute function of HSC measured by CFU assay. In addition, HSC differentiation was measured by complete blood count at five and nine month post Mlh1+/+ and Mlh1+/- mice irradiated with 1H ion or 28Si ion. At these time points, irradiation impact on HSC differentiation was undetermined along with no effects of Mlh1 status on long-term differentiation.
<p>
Investigators found that Mlh1+/+ mice irradiated with any dose or IR source showed no significant increase in the tumor formation due to radiation exposure. In contrast, Mlh1+/- mice showed sensitization to radiation-induced tumorigenesis. They revealed a significant higher incidence of deaths due to tumor formation in Mlh1+/- mice irradiated with 100 cGy 28Si ion IR compared to 100 cGy 1H ion IR (p=0.0001). The finding shows that high-LET IR is more effective in prompting tumorigenesis compared to low-LET 1H ion IR in MMR compromised mice. In addition, histology and immunohistochemistry analysis revealed a majority of tumors in Mlh1+/- mice were lymphomas, in particular T-cell rich B-cell lymphomas. Collectively, the data revealed that incidence of lymphomagenesis in MMR deficient mice was LET dependent.
<p>
The hierarchical clustering analysis revealed that all control age-matched lymph node samples clustered together with a similar gene expression profile. In contrast, TRB lymphomas gene expression profiles were discrete from control lymph nodes and no further distinction was observed between irradiation sources or Mlh1 status of TRB lymphomas. To visualize the similarity and differences between TRB lymphoma cohorts, the investigators plotted the same gene expression profile data by grouping samples to their respective groups. The data showed that irradiated TRB lymphoma cohorts are distinct from sham-irradiated Mlh1+/- cohort, suggesting that tumorigenesis processes associated with irradiation are unique in nature.
<p>
Lastly, the investigators looked at the number of differentially expressed genes in each cohort to identify how many genes are differentially expressed between cohorts. They determined that over 85% of differentially expressed genes are found to be commonly associated with all cohorts, regardless of Mlh1 status or irradiation source. Interestingly, they observed that roughly eight percent of differentially expressed genes are only associated with irradiated cohorts with no correlation found between low- and high-LET IR. Similarly, they also observed that roughly 11% of differentially expressed genes are only found to be associated with sham irradiated cohort. The data suggest that there are common genes involved in the process of tumorigenesis, but enough differences exist between irradiated versus sham-irradiated cohorts that could be the reason for early and higher frequency of tumorigenesis found in 28Si ion irradiated Mlh1+/- mice. In fact, gene ontology analysis revealed that processes, for instance DNA repair, mRNA and rRNA processing, ribosome biogenesis, and RNA splicing, were commonly involved in all TRB lymphoma cohorts. In contrast, differentially regulated genes belong to adherens junction and anchoring junction were predominantly found in irradiated TRB lymphoma cohorts, but not in sham-irradiated TRB lymphoma cohort.
<p>
There are no datasets for this study. A final report was archived.
<p>
